[{:db/id "q-265990/field-1/ca89597554e6cce42715b488e31896548f1b0a99e79b893253f58be435fb95b1" :answer/id #uuid "951c092a-d97b-532c-9924-2b58544b29ec" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1.684" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-265990/field-1/6de51bc8ab21a488789f74f60985f42c77a5e45ed8c031680b5590535e2aa9d3" :answer/id #uuid "d607d054-7ee7-51cf-9821-c566448172c6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1.685" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-265990/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "12b572bc-1860-5db6-8997-593c9fd29842" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-265990/field-1/ca89597554e6cce42715b488e31896548f1b0a99e79b893253f58be435fb95b1" :answer-field/choices ["q-265990/field-1/ca89597554e6cce42715b488e31896548f1b0a99e79b893253f58be435fb95b1" "q-265990/field-1/6de51bc8ab21a488789f74f60985f42c77a5e45ed8c031680b5590535e2aa9d3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-265990" :question/id #uuid "fea4a9c1-07dd-51cd-a1e0-aa68ec9df6ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-265990" :question/problem "Use the first four terms of the binomial expansion of $(1+x)^{5}$ in ascending powers of $x$ to approximate $(1.11)^{5}.$ Round your answer to three decimal places.\n\n$(1.11)^{5}≈$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Recall that when $n$ is a positive integer, \n\n$$\n(1+x)^{n}=1+nx+\\frac{1}{2!}n(n-1){x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3!}n(n-1)(n-2){x}^{3}+⋯+{x}^{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(1+x)}^{5} & =1+5x+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 5\\cdot 4\\cdot {x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 5\\cdot 4\\cdot 3\\cdot {x}^{3}+⋯+{x}^{5} \\\\  & =1+5x+10{x}^{2}+10{x}^{3}+⋯\\,+{x}^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{(1+x)}^{5}≈1+5x+10{x}^{2}+10{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=0.11$ into the above approximation of $(1+x)^{5}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1.11)^{5} & =(1+0.11)^{5} \\\\  & ≈1+5(0.11)+10(0.11)^{2}+10(0.11)^{3} \\\\  & =1+5(0.11)+10(0.012\\,1)+10(0.001\\,331) \\\\  & =1+0.55+0.121+0.013\\,31 \\\\  & ≈1.684,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-265990/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef779b81-341a-59eb-8bd4-860d5d58507c"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-265990"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-134606/selection/04d0361a573a217735babef3e59dc2f9a17125131e6133dc0db53769df7478a7" :answer/id #uuid "acc676e4-d01f-545d-a74d-7927ac992b91" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2.142\\,4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-134606/selection/aa6ec667a2a1fd34371bd6c688cb81d941ff9740b362fcb4b685cc873e200c15" :answer/id #uuid "dea13688-fd6a-5c8b-86a3-51964879c2a6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2.149\\,6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-134606/selection/2021578f54c965f38fe6bc054bd1f0c666d199de8cebabd3cd1ce4e4636aed18" :answer/id #uuid "95979821-7d0f-5285-8126-23dafa1b0bcf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2.210\\,7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-134606/selection/c7f10985858673214626dfd0e9bf942ee051a3fb47912ff78c4dc05d3582ca9a" :answer/id #uuid "c4409990-bc30-5f14-9601-cb03723c91c8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2.145\\,1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-134606/selection/6ec2ee9713c85b0038332addec1b87ef7ac59c15cb19542024353de755874712" :answer/id #uuid "6baaeb36-9b85-5b63-85d9-91ecb2e9cbb3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2.164\\,6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-134606/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "1c6c2e4b-993f-5692-a489-ea347576046e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-134606/selection/04d0361a573a217735babef3e59dc2f9a17125131e6133dc0db53769df7478a7" :answer-field/choices ["q-134606/selection/04d0361a573a217735babef3e59dc2f9a17125131e6133dc0db53769df7478a7" "q-134606/selection/aa6ec667a2a1fd34371bd6c688cb81d941ff9740b362fcb4b685cc873e200c15" "q-134606/selection/2021578f54c965f38fe6bc054bd1f0c666d199de8cebabd3cd1ce4e4636aed18" "q-134606/selection/c7f10985858673214626dfd0e9bf942ee051a3fb47912ff78c4dc05d3582ca9a" "q-134606/selection/6ec2ee9713c85b0038332addec1b87ef7ac59c15cb19542024353de755874712"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-134606" :question/id #uuid "42cfaebd-c61b-5499-96a6-a22612d8275f" :question/math-academy-id "q-134606" :question/problem "Use the first three terms of the binomial expansion of $(1+x)^{7}$ in ascending powers of $x$ to approximate $(1.12)^{7}.$ Give your answer to four decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Recall that when $n$ is a positive integer, \n\n$$\n(1+x)^{n}=1+nx+\\frac{1}{2!}n(n-1){x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(1+x)}^{7} & =1+7x+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 7\\cdot 6\\cdot {x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{7} \\\\  & =1+7x+21{x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{(1+x)}^{7}≈1+7x+21{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=0.12$ into the above approximation of $(1+x)^{7}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1.12)^{7} & =(1+0.12)^{7} \\\\  & ≈1+7(0.12)+21(0.12)^{2} \\\\  & =1+7(0.12)+21(0.014\\,4) \\\\  & =1+0.84+0.302\\,4 \\\\  & =2.142\\,4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto four decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-134606/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef779b81-341a-59eb-8bd4-860d5d58507c"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-134606"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-265899/field-1/0bed16a9f2175327eabb18019d05b65f77586f3f88bf24c1bceb8d217cbd415b" :answer/id #uuid "f86b6293-38e8-5dc7-9ecf-b88b3893069d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3.9175" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-265899/field-1/248f716e457121e8732d692556f1e1d35acb8523ba4fb51ee0c51fa9957ba95f" :answer/id #uuid "218ff919-ebeb-5976-90b9-1a85fcbeea81" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3.9176" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-265899/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "e91eb2f8-2523-5a71-b0d0-7702eca12957" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-265899/field-1/0bed16a9f2175327eabb18019d05b65f77586f3f88bf24c1bceb8d217cbd415b" :answer-field/choices ["q-265899/field-1/0bed16a9f2175327eabb18019d05b65f77586f3f88bf24c1bceb8d217cbd415b" "q-265899/field-1/248f716e457121e8732d692556f1e1d35acb8523ba4fb51ee0c51fa9957ba95f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-265899" :question/id #uuid "afaecf66-0759-5896-86a8-e21ce74eae49" :question/math-academy-id "q-265899" :question/problem "Use the first four terms of the binomial expansion of $(1+x)^{10}$ in ascending powers of $x$ to approximate $(1.15)^{10}.$ Round your answer to four decimal places.\n\n$(1.15)^{10}≈$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Recall that when $n$ is a positive integer, \n\n$$\n(1+x)^{n}=1+nx+\\frac{1}{2!}n(n-1){x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3!}n(n-1)(n-2){x}^{3}+⋯+{x}^{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(1+x)}^{10} & =1+10x+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 10\\cdot 9\\cdot {x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 10\\cdot 9\\cdot 8\\cdot {x}^{3}+⋯+{x}^{10} \\\\  & =1+10x+45{x}^{2}+120{x}^{3}+⋯\\,+{x}^{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{(1+x)}^{10}≈1+10x+45{x}^{2}+120{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=0.15$ into the above approximation of $(1+x)^{10}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1.15)^{10} & =(1+0.15)^{10} \\\\  & ≈1+10(0.15)+45(0.15)^{2}+120(0.15)^{3} \\\\  & =1+10(0.15)+45(0.022\\,5)+120(0.003\\,375) \\\\  & =1+1.5+1.012\\,5+0.405 \\\\  & ≈3.917\\,5,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to four decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-265899/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef779b81-341a-59eb-8bd4-860d5d58507c"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-265899"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-265895/field-1/e68