48" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-266001/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "ae231fd2-23f8-5f50-80aa-35bfc31a8cb0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-266001/field-1/9ace97516ecba3ff708edbf6427154e5861007d55f2ec43b6fbb69784cf8c480" :answer-field/choices ["q-266001/field-1/9ace97516ecba3ff708edbf6427154e5861007d55f2ec43b6fbb69784cf8c480"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-266001" :question/id #uuid "381392d2-7805-5631-8ab9-37792d208dd2" :question/math-academy-id "q-266001" :question/problem "Use the first four terms of the binomial expansion of $(1+x)^{10}$ in ascending powers of $x$ to approximate $(0.84)^{10}.$ Round your answer to five decimal places.\n\n$(0.84)^{10}≈$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Recall that when $n$ is a positive integer, \n\n$$\n(1+x)^{n}=1+nx+\\frac{1}{2!}n(n-1){x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3!}n(n-1)(n-2){x}^{3}+⋯+{x}^{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(1+x)}^{10} & =1+10x+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 10\\cdot 9\\cdot {x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 10\\cdot 9\\cdot 8\\cdot {x}^{3}+⋯+{x}^{10} \\\\  & =1+10x+45{x}^{2}+120{x}^{3}+⋯\\,+{x}^{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{(1+x)}^{10}≈1+10x+45{x}^{2}+120{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=-0.16$ into the above approximation of $(1+x)^{10}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0.84)^{10} & =(1+(-0.16))^{10} \\\\  & ≈1+10(-0.16)+45(-0.16)^{2}+120(-0.16)^{3} \\\\  & =1-10(0.16)+45(0.025\\,6)-120(0.004\\,096) \\\\  & =1-1.6+1.152-0.491\\,52 \\\\  & ≈0.060\\,48\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to five decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-266001/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "13e430ab-a814-5613-a45d-d8cdd3c14a0f"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-266001"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-134609/selection/df41ccbb92e15954c5991378d3f059f0cc404e9dd13f67500a891b9faf754797" :answer/id #