rked-solution "The binomial theorem for positive integer $n$ states that\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{0}){a}^{n}{b}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{1}){a}^{n-1}{b}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{2}){a}^{n-2}{b}^{2}+…+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{n}){a}^{0}{b}^{n}.\n$$\n\nBy substituting $a=4,$ $b=x,$ and $n=4,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(4+x)}^{4} & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{0}){(4)}^{4}{(x)}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{1}){(4)}^{4-1}{(x)}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{2}){(4)}^{4-2}{(x)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1{(4)}^{4}+4{(4)}^{3}{(x)}^{1}+6{(4)}^{2}{(x)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =256+4(64)(x)+6(16)({x}^{2})+⋯ \\\\  & =256+256x+96{x}^{2}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{(4+x)}^{4}≈256+256x+96{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=-0.01$ into the above approximation of $(4+x)^{4}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3.99)^{4} & =(4+(-0.01))^{4} \\\\  & ≈256+256(-0.01)+96(-0.01)^{2} \\\\  & =256-256(0.01)+96(0.000\\,1) \\\\  & =256-2.56+0.009\\,6 \\\\  & =253.450,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto three decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-178357/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4395c989-325f-5ac7-bdb3-f577f18695ed"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-178357"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-134531/selection/5eea20dd15d2a3cca904e645aab275eb1daaf448ac7c2e09abf5cf425d53f50f" :answer/id #uuid "5f062ea8-86a1-5242-b729-54e6c00b860d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.05%" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-134531/selection/91ebc682171ee5c5445a157731cf68599728bed03c3b31a09d9e16f79fbfb505" :answer/id #uuid "dfe285bf-8b87-56a0-8a76-2b48ebdc4038" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.02%" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-134531/selection/a152178c278ee194bae548c8e663a829641ceef521291a811a86ddab57f18dca" :answer/id #uuid "b2ff5825-c31d-5122-927d-b26adebceaa7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.07%" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-134531/selection/5647829872ec140b6283eeda77da3f86ad0a844dff27110f38e8af11e4f75b1c" :answer/id #uuid "efb9bc46-3c46-5225-a62e-db389171615e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.01%" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-134531/selection/6616556259bc1e1299f7f38a37615d157845e40211941a680ed847d757b94b4e" :answer/id #uuid "1bc0e3b9-37ed-5d8c-bbae-ec20fceb291b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.03%" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-134531/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "7dd76774-8dfe-558b-add5-84c24403f423" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-134531/selection/91ebc682171ee5c5445a157731cf68599728bed03c3b31a09d9e16f79fbfb505" :answer-field/choices ["q-134531/selection/5eea20dd15d2a3cca904e645aab275eb1daaf448ac7c2e09abf5cf425d53f50f" "q-134531/selection/91ebc682171ee5c5445a157731cf68599728bed03c3b31a09d9e16f79fbfb505" "q-134531/selection/a152178c278ee194bae548c8e663a829641ceef521291a811a86ddab57f18dca" "q-134531/selection/5647829872ec140b6283eeda77da3f86ad0a844dff27110f38e8af11e4f75b1c" "q-134531/selection/6616556259bc1e1299f7f38a37615d157845e40211941a680ed847d757b94b4e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-134531" :question/id #uuid "ca6e7f72-b383-5353-a2b5-6242ed2f3e39" :question/math-academy-id "q-134531" :question/problem "Calculate the percentage error obtained when approximating the value of $(1.02)^{7}$ using the first three terms of the binomial expansion of $(1+x)^{7}$ in ascending powers of $x.$ Round your final percentage error to two decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Recall that when $n$ is a positive integer, \n\n$$\n(1+x)^{n}=1+nx+\\frac{1}{2!}n(n-1){x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(1+x)}^{7} & =1+7x+\\frac{1}{2!}\\cdot 7\\cdot 6\\cdot {x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{n} \\\\  & =1+7x+21{x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{(1+x)}^{7}≈1+7x+21{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $x=0.02$ into the above approximation of $(1+x)^{7}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1.02)^{7} & =(1+0.02)^{7} \\\\  & ≈1+7(0.02)+21(0.02)^{2} \\\\  & =1+7(0.02)+21(0.000\\,4) \\\\  & =1+0.14+0.008\\,4 \\\\  & =1.148\\,4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using a calculator, we can compute the percentage error, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}%\\,error & =\\frac{\\text{amount of error}}{\\text{exact value}}\\times 100 \\\\  & =\\frac{|(1.02)^{7}-1.148\\,4|}{(1.02)^{7}}\\times 100 \\\\  & ≈0.02%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-134531/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c3e74e4a-8641-54c9-9d9d-c3ca53c94f8f"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-134531"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-134534/selection/3487fc53b13167a0256f969daaf7d30e11a7a82f98e4e45ec18adc1b59a551bf" :answer/id #uuid "823a093b-e4b6-5da4-bd39-c3a5a2a835da" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.1%" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-134534/selection/ad3d639b3b612ff3d2db3b9236975cd4ed06ee1b8648d24614082e8d3da75545" :answer/id #uuid "f2ae461e-8cf9-5b86-a3f9-7b2d1e8d55bd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.3%" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-134534/selection/b9ce0b6377301409bebb5cfd330d2726eb97142164f59ff4c26bfcad4139f434" :answer/id #uuid "1d4dd1d3-a6e0-57a2-a7bb-fbbb58a14e98" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.6%" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-134534/selection/d4c5778bd9828de1d6f90e5b0df4053467e4abf9bd2f2b8a8a46a0d0aa5e0df7" :answer/id #uuid "a6ed37a6-cfa2-5a8b-ae43-b0804a7b546e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.0%" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-134534/selection/7d538c91894950a8641319fee87a9a75d75ec56ef97d6a03451f4d92063d5ec2" :answer/id #uuid "0d8995b2-9908-55d7-af8c-14cdc678f6e8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.4%" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-134534/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "8c01c720-c314-51be-8b80-cc2f2c173445" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-134534/selection/d4c5778bd9828de1d6f90e5b0df4053467e4abf9bd2f2b8a8a46a0d0aa5e0df7" :answer-field/choices ["q-134534/selection/3487fc53b13167a0256f969daaf7d30e11a7a82f98e4e45ec18adc1b59a551bf" "q-134534/selection/ad3d639b3b612ff3d2db3b9236975cd4ed06ee1b8648d24614082e8d3da75545" "q-134534/selection/b9ce0b6377301409bebb5cfd330d2726eb97142164f59ff4c26bfcad4139f434" "q-134534/selection/d4c5778bd9828de1d6f90e5b0df4053467e4abf9bd2f2b8a8a46a0d0aa5e0df7" "q-134534/selection/7d538c91894950a8641319fee87a9a75d75ec56ef97d6a03451f4d92063d5ec2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-134534" :question/id #uuid "0ba96c61-c614-58f4-9cbe-00016a202776" :question/math-academy-id "q-134534" :question/problem "Calculate the percentage error obtained when approximating the value of $(1.98)^{5}$ using the first three terms of the binomial expansion of $(2+x)^{5}$ in ascending powers of $x.$ Round your final percentage error to one decimal place." :question/worked-solution "The binomial theorem for positive integer $n$ states that\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{0}){a}^{n}{b}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{1}){a}^{n-1}{b}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{2}){a}^{n-2}{b}^{2}+…+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{n}){a}^{0}{b}^{n}.\n$$\n\nBy substituting $a=2,$ $b=x,$ and $n=5,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(2+x)}^{5} & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{0}){(2)}^{5}{(x)}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{1}){(2)}^{5-1}{(x)}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{2}){(2)}^{5-2}{(x)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1{(2)}^{5}+5{(2)}^{4}{(x)}^{1}+10{(2)}^{3}{(x)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1(32)+5(16)(x)+10(8)({x}^{2})+⋯ \\\\  & =32+80x+80{x}^{2}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{(2+x)}^{5}≈32+80x+80{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $x=-0.02$ into the above approximation of $(2+x)^{5}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1.98)^{5} & =(2+(-0.02))^{5} \\\\  & ≈32+80(-0.02)+80(-0.02)^{2} \\\\  & =32-80(0.02)+80(0.000\\,4) \\\\  & =32-1.6+0.032 \\\\  & =30.432\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using a calculator, we can compute the percentage error, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}%\\,error & =\\frac{\\text{amount of error}}{\\text{exact value}}\\times 100 \\\\  & =\\frac{|(1.98)^{5}-30.432|}{(1.98)^{5}}\\times 100 \\\\  & ≈0.0%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-134534/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c3e74e4a-8641-54c9-9d9d-c3ca53c94f8f"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-134534"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-18703"] :question/problem "Calculate the percentage error obtained when approximating the value of $(3.2)^{5}$ using the first three terms of the binomial expansion of $(3+x)^{5}$ in ascending powers of $x.$ Round your final percentage error to one decimal place." :question/worked-solution "The binomial theorem for positive integer $n$ states that\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{0}){a}^{n}{b}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{1}){a}^{n-1}{b}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{2}){a}^{n-2}{b}^{2}+…+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{n}){a}^{0}{b}^{n}.\n$$\n\nBy substituting $a=3,$ $b=x,$ and $n=5,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(3+x)}^{5} & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{0}){(3)}^{5}{(x)}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{1}){(3)}^{5-1}{(x)}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{2}){(3)}^{5-2}{(x)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1{(3)}^{5}+5{(3)}^{4}{(x)}^{1}+10{(3)}^{3}{(x)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =243+5(81)(x)+10(27)({x}^{2})+⋯ \\\\  & =243+405x+270{x}^{2}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{(3+x)}^{5}≈243+405x+270{x}^{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $x=0.2$ into the above approximation of $(3+x)^{5}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3.2)^{5} & =(3+0.2)^{5} \\\\  & ≈243+405(0.2)+270(0.2)^{2} \\\\  & =243+405(0.2)+270(0.0\\,4) \\\\  & =243+81+10.8 \\\\  & =334.8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using a calculator, we can compute the percentage error, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}%\\,error & =\\frac{\\text{amount of error}}{\\text{exact value}}\\times 100 \\\\  & =\\frac{|(3.2)^{5}-334.8|}{(3.2)^{5}}\\times 100 \\\\  & ≈0.2%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-134536/selection/6c7c1248b99f4a025f710332f816fd0d113d7d64e92caad3bdb3cdee81f09e85" :answer/id #uuid "66211a9e-8213-5add-ad54-ed4118292a24" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.04%" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-134536/selection/5eea20dd15d2a3cca904e645aab275eb1daaf448ac7c2e09abf5cf425d53f50f" :answer/id #uuid "3ebba384-09db-597a-bcd0-0479fd5961a6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.05%" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-134536/selection/91ebc682171ee5c5445a157731cf68599728bed03c3b31a09d9e16f79fbfb505" :answer/id #uuid "f658969d-a0ed-5e73-94e7-d289e61198e3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.02%" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-134536/selection/5647829872ec140b6283eeda77da3f86ad0a844dff27110f38e8af11e4f75b1c" :answer/id #uuid "c7f4ca61-a0c9-55b5-9ef3-6a34e1acca0f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.01%" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-134536/selection/31fee462d6889f36ecb788dbb7473d519246baef6b5ecdb17282a40d77131740" :answer/id #uuid "f5d87437-0af1-5704-b0ea-dd4ec036a9f6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.00%" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-134536/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "30d8463a-17d8-5334-884e-e816df59abbe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-134536/selection/91ebc682171ee5c5445a157731cf68599728bed03c3b31a09d9e16f79fbfb505" :answer-field/choices ["q-134536/selection/6c7c1248b99f4a025f710332f816fd0d113d7d64e92caad3bdb3cdee81f09e85" "q-134536/selection/5eea20dd15d2a3cca904e645aab275eb1daaf448ac7c2e09abf5cf425d53f50f" "q-134536/selection/91ebc682171ee5c5445a157731cf68599728bed03c3b31a09d9e16f79fbfb505" "q-134536/selection/5647829872ec140b6283eeda77da3f86ad0a844dff27110f38e8af11e4f75b1c" "q-134536/selection/31fee462d6889f36ecb788dbb7473d519246baef6b5ecdb17282a40d77131740"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-134536" :question/id #uuid "9e76bf69-0203-5fbd-82f9-53054708bcbc" :question/math-academy-id "q-134536" :question/problem "Calculate the percentage error obtained when approximating the value of $(2.92)^{5}$ using the first three terms of the binomial expansion of $(3+x)^{5}$ in ascending powers of $x.$ Round your final percentage error to two decimal places." :question/worked-solution "The binomial theorem for positive integer $n$ states that\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{0}){a}^{n}{b}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{1}){a}^{n-1}{b}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{2}){a}^{n-2}{b}^{2}+…+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{n}){a}^{0}{b}^{n}.\n$$\n\nBy substituting $a=3,$ $b=x,$ and $n=5,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(3+x)}^{5} & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{0}){(3)}^{5}{(x)}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{1}){(3)}^{5-1}{(x)}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{2}){(3)}^{5-2}{(x)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1{(3)}^{5}+5{(3)}^{4}{(x)}^{1}+10{(3)}^{3}{(x)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1(243)+5(81)(x)+10(27)({x}^{2})+⋯ \\\\  & =243+405x+270{x}^{2}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{(3+x)}^{5}≈243+405x+270{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $x=-0.08$ into the above approximation of $(3+x)^{5}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2.92)^{5} & =(3+(-0.08))^{5} \\\\  & ≈243+405(-0.08)+270(-0.08)^{2} \\\\  & =243-405(0.08)+270(0.006\\,4) \\\\  & =243-32.4+1.728 \\\\  & =212.328\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using a calculator, we can compute the percentage error, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}%\\,error & =\\frac{\\text{amount of error}}{\\text{exact value}}\\times 100 \\\\  & =\\frac{|(2.92)^{5}-212.328|}{(2.92)^{5}}\\times 100 \\\\  & ≈0.02%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-134536/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c3e74e4a-8641-54c9-9d9d-c3ca53c94f8f"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-134536"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-134533/selection/3a343b253027ccbd6df8a80a4a01f29ab0cc961349bdfe9bbbc95d1daf406e66" :answer/id #uuid "0736794e-9308-5b63-b1b8-a4c92b2840c3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.10%" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-134533/selection/02a0d58824a2681fd8b2117e101d380b97b69a871f8ddb6f5881dcc25c8f9405" :answer/id #uuid "067f586d-8328-53dd-9f2f-83841c2d7983" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.15%" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-134533/selection/a152178c278ee194bae548c8e663a829641ceef521291a811a86ddab57f18dca" :answer/id #uuid "756d4602-b8b3-5195-bd0f-149def339e8d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.07%" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-134533/selection/fbe382b144ba2c123b4bbb3387fb630828b9dc35a277be6f3feca439ebf11d31" :answer/id #uuid "30a197bd-b0fc-5cd8-870b-e81ac5b79265" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.12%" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-134533/selection/f943f7c20316b078a7ad952abec8f9196a0cdd9b060d312424d577963284f2e5" :answer/id #uuid "3daf8d9a-b289-561f-94f3-4d1d1a23caec" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.08%" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-134533/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "8c8ae942-23ef-5701-9bcc-d0e8ecdb8de5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-134533/selection/3a343b253027ccbd6df8a80a4a01f29ab0cc961349bdfe9bbbc95d1daf406e66" :answer-field/choices ["q-134533/selection/3a343b253027ccbd6df8a80a4a01f29ab0cc961349bdfe9bbbc95d1daf406e66" "q-134533/selection/02a0d58824a2681fd8b2117e101d380b97b69a871f8ddb6f5881dcc25c8f9405" "q-134533/selection/a152178c278ee194bae548c8e663a829641ceef521291a811a86ddab57f18dca" "q-134533/selection/fbe382b144ba2c123b4bbb3387fb630828b9dc35a277be6f3feca439ebf11d31" "q-134533/selection/f943f7c20316b078a7ad952abec8f9196a0cdd9b060d312424d577963284f2e5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-134533" :question/id #uuid "201de3ad-c6dc-5dd3-8566-2c0956be1132" :question/math-academy-id "q-134533" :question/problem "Calculate the percentage error obtained when approximating the value of $(2.1)^{5}$ using the first three terms of the binomial expansion of $(2+x)^{5}$ in ascending powers of $x.$ Round your final percentage error to two decimal places." :question/worked-solution "The binomial theorem for positive integer $n$ states that\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{0}){a}^{n}{b}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{1}){a}^{n-1}{b}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{2}){a}^{n-2}{b}^{2}+…+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{n}){a}^{0}{b}^{n}.\n$$\n\nBy substituting $a=2,$ $b=x,$ and $n=5,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(2+x)}^{5} & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{0}){(2)}^{5}{(x)}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{1}){(2)}^{5-1}{(x)}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{2}){(2)}^{5-2}{(x)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1{(2)}^{5}+5{(2)}^{4}{(x)}^{1}+10{(2)}^{3}{(x)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1(32)+5(16)(x)+10(8)({x}^{2})+⋯ \\\\  & =32+80x+80{x}^{2}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{(2+x)}^{5}≈32+80x+80{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $x=0.1$ into the above approximation of $(2+x)^{5}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2.1)^{5} & =(2+0.1)^{5} \\\\  & ≈32+80(0.1)+80(0.1)^{2} \\\\  & =32+80(0.1)+80(0.01) \\\\  & =32+8+0.8 \\\\  & =40.8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using a calculator, we can compute the percentage error, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}%\\,error & =\\frac{\\text{amount of error}}{\\text{exact value}}\\times 100 \\\\  & =\\frac{|(2.1)^{5}-40.8|}{(2.1)^{5}}\\times 100 \\\\  & ≈0.10%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-134533/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c3e74e4a-8641-54c9-9d9d-c3ca53c94f8f"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-134533"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-13020"] :question/problem "Use the first four terms of the binomial expansion of $(1+x)^6$ in ascending powers of $x$ to approximate $(1.15)^6.$ Give your answer to three decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Recall that when $n$ is a positive integer, \n\n$$\n(1+x)^n = 1 + nx + \\dfrac{1}{2!} n(n-1)x^2 + \\dfrac{1}{3!} n(n-1)(n-2)x^3 + \\cdots + x^n.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(1+x)}^{6} & =1+6x+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 6\\cdot 5\\cdot {x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 6\\cdot 5\\cdot 4\\cdot {x}^{3}+⋯+{x}^{n} \\\\  & =1+6x+15{x}^{2}+20{x}^{3}+⋯\\,+{x}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\left( {1 + x} \\right)^6 \\approx 1 + 6x + 15x^2 + 20x^3.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=0.15$ into the above approximation of $(1 + x)^6{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1.15)^{6} & =(1+0.15)^{6} \\\\  & ≈1+6(0.15)^{1}+15(0.15)^{2}+20(0.15)^{3} \\\\  & =1+6(0.15)+15(0.022\\,5)+20(0.003\\,375) \\\\  & =1+0.9+0.337\\,5+0.067\\,5 \\\\  & =2.305,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto three decimal places." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-13021"] :question/problem "Use the first four terms of the binomial expansion of $(1+x)^8$ in ascending powers of $x$ to approximate $(0.89)^8.$ Give your answer to six decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Recall that when $n$ is a positive integer, \n\n$$\n(1+x)^n = 1 + nx + \\dfrac{1}{2!} n(n-1)x^2 + \\dfrac{1}{3!} n(n-1)(n-2)x^3 + \\cdots + x^n.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(1+x)}^{8} & =1+8x+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 8\\cdot 7\\cdot {x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 8\\cdot 7\\cdot 6\\cdot {x}^{3}+⋯+{x}^{n} \\\\  & =1+8x+28{x}^{2}+56{x}^{3}+⋯\\,+{x}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\left( {1 + x} \\right)^8 \\approx 1 + 8x + 28x^2 + 56x^3.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=-0.11$ into the above approximation of $(1 + x)^8{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0.89)^{8} & =(1+(-0.11))^{8} \\\\  & ≈1+8(-0.11)^{1}+28(-0.11)^{2}+56(-0.11)^{3} \\\\  & =1-8(0.11)+28(0.012\\,1)-56(0.001\\,331) \\\\  & =1-0.88+0.338\\,8-0.074\\,536 \\\\  & =0.384\\,264,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto six decimal places." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-13022"] :question/problem "Use the first $3$ terms of the binomial expansion of ${\\left( 2+x \\right)^{4}}$ in ascending powers of $x$ to approximate $(1.97)^{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "The binomial theorem for positive integer $n$ states that\n\n \n\n$$\n{(a + b)^n} = {n \\choose 0}{a^n}{b^0} + {n \\choose 1}{a^{n - 1}}{b^1} + {n \\choose 2}{a^{n - 2}}{b^2} +  \\cdots  + {n \\choose n}{a^0}{b^n}.\n$$\n\nBy substituting $a=2,$ $b=x,$ and $n=4,$ we obtain\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(2+x)}^{4}= & (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{0}){(2)}^{4}{(x)}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{1}){(2)}^{4-1}{(x)}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{2}){(2)}^{4-2}{(x)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1\\cdot {(2)}^{4}{(x)}^{0}+4{(2)}^{3}{(x)}^{1}+6{(2)}^{2}{(x)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =16+4(8)(x)+6(4)({x}^{2})+⋯ \\\\  & =16+32x+24{x}^{2}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\left( {2 + x} \\right)^{4} \\approx 16 + 32x + 24{x^2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=-0.03$ into the expansion of $(2+x)^4{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1.97)^{4} & =(2-0.03)^{4} \\\\  & ≈16+32(-0.03)+24(-0.03)^{2} \\\\  & =16-0.96+0.021\\,6 \\\\  & =15.0616\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-13023"] :question/problem "Calculate the percentage error obtained when approximating the value of $(1.08)^{5}$ using the first three terms of the binomial expansion of $(1+x)^5$ in ascending powers of $x.$ Round your final percentage error to two decimal places." :question/worked-solution "The binomial theorem for positive integer $n$ states that\n\n$$\n{(a + b)^n} = {n \\choose 0}{a^n}{b^0} + {n \\choose 1}{a^{n - 1}}{b^1} + {n \\choose 2}{a^{n - 2}}{b^2} +  \\ldots  + {n \\choose n}{a^0}{b^n}.\n$$\n\nBy substituting $a = 1,$ $b = x,$ and $n = 5,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(1+x)}^{5} & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{0}){(1)}^{5}{(x)}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{1}){(1)}^{5-1}{(x)}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{2}){(1)}^{5-2}{(x)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1{(1)}^{5}+5{(1)}^{4}{(x)}^{1}+10{(1)}^{3}{(x)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1+5(1)(x)+10(1)({x}^{2})+⋯ \\\\  & =1+5x+10{x}^{2}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\left( {1 + x} \\right)^{5}} \\approx 1 + 5x + 10x^2.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $x = 0.08$ into the above approximation of $(1 + x)^5{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1.08)^{5} & =(1+0.08)^{5} \\\\  & ≈1+5(0.08)+10(0.08)^{2} \\\\  & =1+5(0.08)+10(0.006\\,4) \\\\  & =1+0.4+0.064 \\\\  & =1.464\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using a calculator, we can compute the percentage error, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}%\\,error & =\\frac{\\text{amount of error}}{\\text{exact value}}\\times 100 \\\\  & =\\frac{|(1.08)^{5}-1.464|}{(1.08)^{5}}\\times 100 \\\\  & ≈0.36%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
